-Snapshot validation, backend preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. The lookup backend is not selected yet. Candidates are a local embedded SQLite index, an on-site HTTPS directory, and a managed search service. No certificate behavior or interface change is part of that selection.
+The lookup backend is a local embedded SQLite index, private to this node. Each kiosk owns its index file and rebuilds it when a snapshot arrives; no external service or network participates in validation, rebuild, or lookup. Snapshot validation, index preparation, and lookup all belong to this node.
 
 ## Snapshot Import
 
-Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Prepare the new snapshot without exposing partially imported records. Activate it only after all validation succeeds. An import failure leaves the previous snapshot active and reports a useful error.
+Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Build the new index into a staging SQLite database alongside the active one, then activate it by atomic rename only after the index is fully built, so no partially imported records are ever visible. An import failure at any stage — signature, schema version, uniqueness, or a failed index build — discards the staging database, leaves the previous snapshot active, and reports a useful error.
 
 ## Lookup Behavior
 
-Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists. The concrete index and query path remain open pending the user's choice.
+Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Records are stored with a normalized serial column and an index over it, so each lookup is a single indexed equality query against the active database. The active snapshot date is stored as database metadata and returned with every result, including NotFound.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Lookup backend | Local embedded SQLite index | On-site HTTPS directory; managed search service | The local index is the only option consistent with the offline-lookup tenet: no runtime network route, no service to operate, and predictable kiosk-side lookups. The cost — each kiosk validates and rebuilds its own index when a snapshot arrives — stays inside this node and leaves the certificate interface unchanged. |
